//? Title: Select groups and individual slopes along a regularization path
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare group and within-group selection

Four predictors belong to two groups. Each group contains a strong
predictor and a weaker one. Group lasso penalizes each group's
Euclidean norm. Sparse group lasso adds an absolute-value penalty
on each slope, so it can retain a group and set a weak slope to zero.

```{cpp, id=group-lasso-path, file="figures/group_lasso_path.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/group_lasso.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    linalg::DenseMatrix<double> x(8, 4);
    std::vector<double> y(8);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < y.size(); ++i) {
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
            x(i, j) = i & (1u << j) ? 1 : -1;
        x(i, 3) = x(i, 0) * x(i, 1);
        y[i] = 10 + 2.6*x(i, 0) + .25*x(i, 1) + 1.2*x(i, 2) + .08*x(i, 3);
    }
    stats::GroupLassoOptions options{.lambdas = {1.8, 1, .65, .3, .15}};
    const auto group = stats::group_lasso_path(x, y, {10,10,20,20}, options);
    options.alpha = .55;
    const auto sparse = stats::group_lasso_path(x, y, {10,10,20,20}, options);
    std::vector<double> penalty, weak_group, weak_sparse;
    std::println("| Penalty | Groups with group lasso | Groups with sparse group lasso |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < group.size(); ++i) {
        if (!group[i].converged || !sparse[i].converged)
            throw std::runtime_error("Expected both regularization paths to converge");
        std::println("| {:.2f} | {} | {} |", group[i].lambda, group[i].active_groups.size(),
                     sparse[i].active_groups.size());
        penalty.push_back(group[i].lambda);
        weak_group.push_back(group[i].coefficients[1]);
        weak_sparse.push_back(sparse[i].coefficients[1]);
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    graphics::RPlot::create()
        .line(penalty, weak_group, "Group lasso", {29, 78, 216}, 2.5)
        .line(penalty, weak_sparse, "Sparse group lasso", {180, 83, 9}, 2.5)
        .title("A weak slope in a retained group")
        .x_label("Penalty strength").y_label("Slope for predictor 2")
        .size(740, 420).save_svg("build/group_lasso_path.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Penalty | Groups with group lasso | Groups with sparse group lasso |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1.80 | 1 | 1 |
| 1.00 | 1 | 2 |
| 0.65 | 2 | 2 |
| 0.30 | 2 | 2 |
| 0.15 | 2 | 2 |
\image(figures/group_lasso_path.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Sparse group lasso drops the weak slope at a lower penalty strength.)
\alttext(Both fitted slopes shrink as the penalty rises. The sparse group lasso line reaches zero by 0.65, while the group lasso line stays positive through 1.8.)

At penalty 0.65, both fits retain the first group. The group-lasso fit
keeps a small slope for its weaker predictor. The sparse group-lasso
fit sets that slope to zero.

[Yuan and Lin, Model Selection and Estimation in Regression with Grouped Variables|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2005.00532.x]
[Simon et al., A Sparse-Group Lasso|https://doi.org/10.1080/10618600.2012.681250]
